1 OCCU Rektor i Kolding, 1636 Præst i Seem. Sognepræst i Ribe ved Sct. Cathrinæ Kirke, snere Provst i Ribe, Ribe Domkirke, 1648 præst i Ribe Sct. Cathrine
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Updated from [http://trees.ancestry.com/tree/59016579/person/48350478277 Ancestry Genealogy] via son [http://www.geni.com/profile-34679970163 John Fost of England and Dover Tailor Attorney] by [http://www.geni.co
2 CONC m/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 3 2015, 15:13:39 UTC'' 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT David var ikke far til John Foss og han har så vidt vides heller aldrig været i USA
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===outdated genealogy===
2 CONT
2 CONT Extracted from Biography and Genealogy of Western Massachusetts, 1639-1925: The Foss family in America belonged to the nobility of Norway, bearing a coat-of-arms, the chief figure in both arms and crest being that of a fox. The na
2 CONC me was originally Vos, which signifies fox, and was pronounced foss. The line comes through Denmark and England to America, and the first of whom any knowledge has been traced was a man named Lauritz. According to the custom of th
2 CONC e age in that country, the name of the son was adopted from the baptismal name of the father.
2 CONT
2 CONT David Lauritzen Foss, born in Norway in 1604, removed to Denmark when a young man, and died at Ribe, in that country, August 31, 1659. He was a minister of the gospel and was pastor of St. Catherine's Church at Ribe, in 1648; he w
2 CONC as also a magistrate and afterwards provost at Ribe. He married there, September 10, 1636, Jansdatter Hundevard, born February 15, 1620 died September 16, 1684, daughter of Jens Lorenson and Catherine (Hasdatter) Hundevard. These 
2 CONC records have been obtained from Denmark, and are a feature of the Foss genealogy in manuscript, now in the possession of the New England Historic Genealogical Society. Children born at Ribe: John, of whom further; Karine, Lauritz,
2 CONC  Agatha, Magdalen, Lauritz Davidson, Jene, Antonius, Peter and Inger.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Foss, American immigrant ancestor, son of David Lauritzen and Jansdatter (Hundevard) Foss, was baptized January 3, 1639, in Denmark and became a seafaring man. Going to England with his brother Peter, he entered the British N
2 CONC avy as a ship caulker. On the arrival of the vessel in which he sailed in the port of Boston, he decided to remain in this country and tradition states that he went overboard in the evening and swam ashore. To avoid a forcible ret
2 CONC urn to the service he immediately proceeded to the interior, and shortly settled in Dover, New Hampshire. He purchased a house in Exeter, New Hampshire, September 29, 1666, and sold it in April, 1671. In 1667, 1669, 1672 and 1688 
2 CONC he was a juror in Dover and for some time lived in Rye, New Hampshire. His will was dated at Dover, December 17, 1699, and he died before January of the following year. He married (first) Mary Chadbourne, born in 1644, daughter of
2 CONC  William and Jane Chadbourne. Her father came to America in the employ of Captain John Mason, to build a mill at South Berwick, Maine, and in 1657 was at Portsmouth, New Hampshire. With him, John Foss removed to Old Kittery, Maine
2 CONC , soon after settling in Dover. He was a prominent man in town affairs in Dover, and was married there (second) by John Wincool, a justice of the peace, January 25, 1686, to Mary (Fernicide) Goss, widow of James Goss. He married (
2 CONC third) Elizabeth (Berry) Lock, daughter of William and Jane Berry, and widow of John Lock, who was killed on Dover Plains, June 26, 1696. His children were: John, Samuel, Joshua, Elizabeth, Mary, William, Walter, Hannah, Thomas, H
2 CONC inkson, killed June 26, 1696; Humphrey, Jemima and Samuel.  These children are descendents of many lines of this old family, scattered now in many States of the Union.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://ghostshipisadore.blogspot.com/2010/11/ancestry-of-leander-foss.html
