0 @N6000000004533564228@ NOTE ONE OF THE MOST INTERESTING and most discussed figures among thebishops of Lund in modern times is Peder Pedersen Winstrup. For morethan forty years he controlled his large diocese devotedly and ablyoften under the most difficult 
1 CONC conditions, half of the time as aDanish and then as a Swedish subject. The present study is concernedwith describing the life and works of this man, who was remarkable inmany respects, up to the Peace of Roskilde in 1658, and plac
1 CONC ing him inthe ecclesiastical, cultural and political milieu in which he livedand worked.
1 CONT It is unanimously agreed that Peder Pedersen Winstrup was born inCopenhagen on April 30th, 1605. His parents were the then bishop ofthe diocese of Zealand and professor of theology at CopenhagenUniversity, Peder Jensen Winstrup an
1 CONC d his wife, Anna Eisenberg. Hismother's family was of German descent. During the period of unrest inGermany in the middle of the 16th century, his maternal grandfatherElias Eisenberg had emigrated from Halle to Denmark, and there 
1 CONC enteredthe service of the Danish king. In his new position he succeeded inbeing entrusted with many responsible offices, and gained greatinfluence. By his wife, Thale Holst, also presumable of German birth,E. Eisenberg had four so
1 CONC ns and two daughters, Marina and Anna, thelatter of whom, as already mentioned, married Bishop P. J. Winstrup.
1 CONT Peder's family on his father's side can trace back to the end of theMiddle Ages. His paternal grandfather, Jens or Johannes Winstrup, bornin 1502, devoted himself to the pursuit of learning and entered thepriesthood during the Cat
1 CONC holic period. Subsequently he went over theLutheran side and became the first Lutheran parish priest of theChurch of St Nicholas in Copenhagen. By his wife, Anna Hofvet, thedaughter of a merchant he had two children, one son and o
1 CONC ne daughter.The son, Peder Jensen Winstrup, was born on March 18th, 1549. Afterstudying for many years in universities both at home and abroad, hemade rapid progress and in 1590 became bishop of the diocese ofZealand. He was witho
1 CONC ut doubt a man of considerable scholarship and ofsome, if formal, talent. He fulfilled his high office well on thewhole, at least during the earlier part of his work. Because of acertain weakness of character, however, he can hard
1 CONC ly be counted asone of the more notable holders of the ancient bishopric of Zealand.His peace -loving and easy-going nature allowed him quit often to beled by stronger personalities, so that his conduct was somewhatvacillating. Bi
1 CONC shop P. J. Winstrup consequently lacked the qualitieswhich are the mark of a great personality. In one sphere, that ofeconomics, he showed himself to posses not inconsiderable talent andhe became in time very well off. From his ma
1 CONC rriage with Anna Eisenberghe had several children. His eldest son, Peder Pedersen Winstrup, isthe one whose destiny is the subject for this study.
1 CONT Peder spent the first twelve years of his life at home, where hismother gave him his first instruction in piety and good behaviour. Hisfather, however, who died as early as 1614 after a serious illnesslasting several years, could 
1 CONC hardly have taken an active part in hiseducation. On the other hand, a strong influence on the spiritualdevelopment of the boy was exercised by the domineering and stubbornHans Poulsen Resen, whom his mother married in November, 1
1 CONC 615.
1 CONT By this time Peder had reached school age and was sent to Roskilde tocontinue his studies there. His stay in the ancient cathedral town wasbrief, however, for at the beginning of 1618 his parents had theopportunity of entering him
1 CONC  at the Royal Free School at Sorö, whichnow became his home for the following five years. It is certain thathis stay in Sorö was of the greatest importance for Peder, and that heobtained there the best book learning available at t
1 CONC hat time inDenmark. He himself has also pronounced in his "Epigrammata" his greatindebtedness to the school for what it gave him.
1 CONT After the end of his schooling Peder returned to Copenhagen to try toenter the University. He matriculated there on May 29th, 1623, duringthe chancellorship of Hans Jensen Hans, and immediately started hisstudies, which comprised 
1 CONC at first classical languages, rhetoric anddialectic. The full course at the academy, planned to be completed intwo or three years, required participation in all the four faculties,however. It can also: be considered certain that, 
1 CONC Peder followed theregulations in this matter. Thus he praises equally the professor ofLaw, Claus Plum, and the professor of Medicine, Caspar Bartholin, oneof the most  distinguished teachers at the University.  He .seems tohave ta
1 CONC ken a special interest in the lectures within the theologicalfaculty, where Hans Poulsen Resen's particular type of orthodoxy withits
1 CONT links with both Luther and Melanchthon had uncontested supremacy afterthe successfully fought struggles against Calvinism during the firsttwo decades of the 17th Century. The stubborn bishop also held officeas the second professor
1 CONC  in the faculty, while his friend andfellow-fighter Jesper Brochmand acted as "summus theologus".
1 CONT Peder, however, did not complete his full two years. A severe plaguehad broken out in Copenhagen at the beginning of 1625. As the epidemicshowed no signs of abating, a royal command was issued in May that theUniversity should clos
1 CONC e. Under these circumstances his parents thoughtit advisable to send him abroad to continue his studies.  Hisdeparture from Denmark took place in the spring of 1625, and hearrived in Wittenberg in June. At this time there were her
1 CONC e richopportunities for those who wanted to deepen their knowledge, for theUniversity could pride itself on a number of distinguished teachers.Peder had such famous men as Volfgang Frantz, Friedrich Balduin,Balthasar Meisner, Paul
1 CONC  Röber and Wilhelm Leyser as his tutors in thetheological faculty, and through them he was able to become acquaintedwith an orthodoxy stamped with moderation and comparatively greattoleration. From them he learned also a closer kn
1 CONC owledge of theremarkable reformation movement for the revival of Christian life,which originated in Germany immediately after the turn of the centuryand had already spread widely over great parts of the country. Hisattention, howe
1 CONC ver, was not entirely given to theology. Peder himselfrelates that his studies also included philology and philosophy.Philology especially had distinguished representatives at Wittenbergin Erasmus Schmidt and August Buchner. The f
1 CONC ormer seems to have beenthe one who, of all the professors at the University, had most to givethe young knowledge-seeking bishop's son.
1 CONT After about five years of concentrated and profitable studies at"Leucorea" he travelled on via Leipzig to Jcna, where the University,thanks mainly to John Gerhard, was at this time in the middle of aperiod of great prosperity. It 
1 CONC must therefore have been considered agreat honour for Peder that the celebrated scholar took him as hisguest into his home, where he had the opportunity of daily associationwith his host and free access to his copious library .Thr
1 CONC oughlectures, discussions and private conversation he was initiated intoGerhard's world of ideas. Not least important was the fact that he gotto l(now his views on ecclesiastical and political law, expressed bythe great theologian
1 CONC  in his main work '(Loci theologici" published tenyears before. Humanistic studies went hand in hand with his study oftheology, and they mainly concerned practical philosophy, logic,metaphysics, physics and rhetoric, in all of whi
1 CONC ch subjects theUniversity had capable lecturers.
1 CONT In the autumn of 1632 the political situation became such that warincidents seemed to be imminent in the Saxony area, and Peder resolvedto leave Jena. His immediate plans were to visit Italy, France andEngland. At the insistent re
1 CONC quest of his parents, however, he changedhis plans and set out on the return trip to his native country andhome, where he arrived in the late of 1632. Before he left, he hadhanded in for printing his "Epigrammata", which, like his
1 CONC  laterwritings, bears witness to his remarkable knowledge of languages,mastership of forrn and wide reading.
1 CONT When he had corne home, Peder succeeded in getting his parents toagree to his cancelled visit to Western Europe. His plans were,however, once again to fall through as a result of intervention fromthe highest quarter, A royal lette
1 CONC r of Nov, 22nd, 1632, commanded theUniversity to allow Peder Winstrup to dispute "pro cathedra" for theprofessorship of Physics. He was ready with his professorial thesis asearly as January, 1633, and this was subjected to public 
1 CONC disputationon the 26th of that mouth. The paper, which is a contribution to thediscussion on the relationship between philosophy and theology, mustbe considered a purely ordinary school work, which can hardly be saidto be superior
1 CONC  to similar works of the day. Two days later the newlyappointed professor took the prescribed oath before the Senate and wasthereby ready to start his teaching work. This, however, was to be ofonly very slight extent, because lect
1 CONC ures were suspended during mostof the short time he held office at the University.
1 CONT At the academy, Peder Winstrup had the opportunity of enlarging hisown knowledge in various subjects. He came now into contact withProfessor Ole Worm, who understood so well how to fire his enthusiasmfor antiquarian research that 
1 CONC Peder kept up his particular interest inthis branch of learning cven after he had relinquished hisprofessorial office. He remained in close contact with Worm as long asthe latter was alive. Winstrup showed no small interest in the
1 CONC Gallehus gold horn, discovered in 1539. Thus he produced a work on thesubject, "Cornicen Danicus", in which he gives an anti-Swedishsymbolic interpretation of the markings on the horn. But his studieshad other subjects than antiqu
1 CONC arian research. He had to acquire thenecessary knowledge to obtain the degree of Master of Arts, whichrequired for academic teaching. This does not seem  to have presentedany considerable difficulty, since he gained the degree as 
1 CONC early asMay 28th, 1633.
1 CONT At about the same time Winstrup was entrusted with the task ofexamining the writings of the well-known court astrologer and almanacmaker Niels Heldevad, and this he performed in a manner both discreetand happy for the elderly auth
1 CONC or. Shortly afterwards he was givenanother similar task. It concerned a comedy this time, entitled"Judith and Hollophernes", which some German refugee students atCopenhagen University wanted to produce.  After only a week the cens
1 CONC orwas ready with his report, which in this case also bore the stamp ofunderstanding and indulgence.
1 CONT Winstrup's most time-consuming commission was in connection with theinspection of the text books of the "Latin schools" ordered by thegovernment in 1633.  It fell to his lot to revise the Latin grammartogether with four colleagues
1 CONC , and alone to review the Greek. He nevercompleted this task, however, because Christian lV,  impressed by thesermons which Winstrup, like the other professors of theology andphilosophy, gave at the castle, appointed him court cha
1 CONC plain Feb.18th, 1635.
1 CONT He was well suited for this high and responsible position because ofhis impressive appearance, great talents, education and diplomaticability. The chances of a confiding relationship between the king andhis spiritual guide were al
1 CONC so great, since both had the same firmtrust in God in the many vicissitudes of life, the same conviction ofthe need for uniformity of doctrine and liturgy within the country,the same lively interest in the moral and spiritual impr
1 CONC ovement of theclergy. From the first Christian IV regarded his young court chaplainwith the greatest goodwill. As a result of daily contact with him forthee years he learnt to value all the more his ability as a preacherand spirit
1 CONC ual guide, his zeal and great serviceableness in differenttasks, his piety and irreproachable mode of life. The appreciation wasmutual. Winstrup for his part found in his royal patron a man on thewhole after his own heart. It was 
1 CONC with pleasure that he observed theattention and devotion which the king showed at services, and he wasproved by his sincere penitence and great meekness at theconfessional. They had hours of intense enjoyment together in Biblestud
1 CONC y. On these occasions there were certainly possibilities for theyoung court to make his voice heard in wordly affairs, becauseChristian IV made it his practice to consult the Scriptures even inpolitical matters.
1 CONT In addition to his tasks as spiritual guide, the court chaplain had,however, to subject himself to the conditions of the oath he took whenhe entered office. In accordance with his oath it was incumbent on himamong other things, un
1 CONC fettered by existing laws, to notify the kingdirect as soon as any false doctrine occurred in any ecclesiastic,whoever it might be. In fact, he thus assumed the role of a sort ofattorney general in matters of orthodoxy. It was als
1 CONC o in his capacityas guardian of religion that Winstrup was drawn into the protractedand bitter doctrinal struggle about Holger Rosenkrantz, in which hecame to play an important role. As far as can be judged, he adopted aconciliato
1 CONC ry and partly appreciative attitude towards the learnednobleman even though he criticized particular points in his doctrineof justification.
1 CONT It is clear that Winstrup should not be without visible marks ofgratitude for his zealous and able execution of the office of courtchaplain. Thus, in 1636 he was called by order of the king to bepromoted doctor of theology at Cope
1 CONC nhagen University. But the royalindulgence also took the form of financial favors, particularly in theshape of a canonry in the Chapter of Lund. It was probably also on therecommendation of Christian IV that he was elected bishop 
1 CONC of thediocese of Lund in succession to Mads Jensen Medelfar at the beginningof 1638.
1 CONT He was consecrated to this high office on May 27th of the same year byBishop Hans Mickelsen of Odense before a great and representativeassembly containing numerous members of the Scanian nobility. From thevery beginning, the 33-ye
1 CONC ar-old bishop undertook his many and greattasks with zeal and energy. During his extensive diocesan tours hecame into personal contact with his numerous clergy and obtainedmoreover a close knowledge of the conditions in the parish
1 CONC es.
1 CONT The experience he thus gained was communicated to the deans at theannual synods "landemoder" in the form of so-called "monita", i. e.instructions and exhortations on what was to be encouraged and whatdiscouraged in the community l
1 CONC ife. These diocesan synods, which wereintended almost as a sort of ecclesiastical law court, became as aresult of their shaping by Wintrup, an effective means of raising thereligious and ethical standard of the clergy, for there w
1 CONC ere seriousshortcomings in this respect. Time after time the bishop came to knowhow now one, now another of his subordinates was lacking in hispersonal mode of life, or in the administration of his office. Theclergy against whom s
1 CONC uch charges were raised, were called to accountby the bishop and, when necessary, punished. If the misdemeanors wereof a milder nature, earnest and affectionate admonitions and warningssufficed. If these did not help, or if the fa
1 CONC ult was of a more seriousnature, stronger measures were adopted, such as the infliction offines, public apologies, suspension for a certain period, and, as afinal resort, dismissal. If  Winstrup did not hesitate to employ evenstro
1 CONC nger forms of punishment, it must however be pointed out that hewas in no way a dogmatist, but on the whole showed great leniencytowards erring clergy. Nor are examples lacking when he even protectedhis subordinates from the conse
1 CONC quences of past offences. Anothersphere in which his consideration for the clergy can be seen, is thefinancial one. Severe taxes, the ravages of war, the thriftiness ofthe nobility, accidental fires, poor harvests and time after t
1 CONC ime therecurrence cattle diseases had seriously undermined the financialposition of the clergy. The consequence was a series of petitions tothe government about relaxation of taxes and increases in enrolments.An examination of the
1 CONC se petitions shows that Winstrup did all thatstood in his power to achieve alleviation of the distress. Meanwhile.he did not content
1 CONT himself with merely supporting the petitions which came in from theclergy, but on several occasions took the initiative himself withsuccess. A fine trait which bears witness to the goodness of hisheart, was also the generosity whi
1 CONC ch he continually showed to widowsof clergy and orphaned children.
1 CONT The solicitude he showed was appreciated. and repaid. The clergylooked up with reverence, confidence and devotion to the learned andconsiderate leader of the diocese, with whom it was easy to obtain anaudience and gain support, if
1 CONC  only one showed due reverence and wascareful not to trespass on his authority. For the bishop stoodverymuch on his dignity and would nor tolerate a subordinate trying toact like his equal. This appeared most clearly in the Chapte
1 CONC r duringthe bitter conflicts with Professor I. Wandell and the parish priestHans Jensen Viborg.
1 CONT Since, as already pointed out, the movement for uniformity which wasin several respects a mark of orthodoxy, had an enthusiastic championin Winstrup, it is quite natural that he should not content himselfwith watching over orthodo
1 CONC xy within his diocese but should also workto achieve a universal similarity in all acts of worship. Thisconcerned particularly the actual services, in which the greatestdisconformities seems to have existed. By emendation and addi
1 CONC tion healso dealt with the question of other church activities as soon asanomalies were noticed.  With the same zeal he also saw to it that theinhabitants of his diocese rook full advantage of the possibilitiesoffered them for the
1 CONC  promotion of their salvation. To make sure ofachieving his aim, he ordained that in every congregation a bookshould be procured in which specially selected persons should notedown every Sunday those who were absent from the servi
1 CONC ce without validexcuse and those who went to communion.
1 CONT These measures, which bear witness to Winstrup's practicaldisposition, were certainly judicious, for ecclesiastical life in manyways left much to be desired, and severe church discipline wastherefore needed. On the other hand, it 
1 CONC was most unsatisfactory thatthose men to whom the above mentioned control was entrusted, shouldreceive part of the fine which was inflicted as a result of theirreports. But it was not only the neglect in the matter of attendanceat
1 CONC  church and communion that became the subject of Winstrup'sintervention. With his usual vigor he saw to it that all theconditions of the so-called church discipline edict were strictlyapplied.  ln his capacity as vice-dean he also
1 CONC  exerted a decisiveinfluence over the management of the so-called marriage suits.
1 CONT Of all the tasks which were entrusted to Winstrup in his capacity ashead of the diocese, none could have afforded him with such greatsatisfaction as the superintendence of education. With his eyes opento the dangers which the Cath
1 CONC olic reaction and to some extent alsoCalvinism had brought to his country, he seems to have considered itperhaps his most important task to work to the best of his ability forthe consolidation of Lutheranism. It therefore appeared
1 CONC  to him anecessity to make the educational system effective, and thus appliednot a little interest to the subject of elementary education. Againand again he reminded parents, parish-clerks and clergy of theirresponsibility for tea
1 CONC ching their children the basic principles ofChristianity, and he did not neglect to give instructions about themethod and the text books which should be used in the impartation ofknowledge.
1 CONT During his diocesan visits he checked carefully that the directivesprepared by him were followed and took the opportunity also ofarranging the practical measures which he considered advisable forachieving the best possible results
1 CONC  by means of the teaching given bythe clergy and parish-clerks.
1 CONT If elementary education therefore enjoyed the continual care ofWinstrup, it was higher education that was the subject of his keenestinterest. All his work on problems of schooling bears the stamp of hiszeal, ability and initiative
1 CONC . It is only to be regretted that hisimpulsiveness and quick temper sometimes caused him to lose control ofhimself with his subordinates. He gave special attention to therecruitment of teachers. This was indeed a very serious prob
1 CONC lem atthis time in Denmark, when both headmasters and teachers weregenerally ill paid and enjoyed only small social standing. As often ascircumstances allowed, the educationally-minded bishop went on toursof inspection to the diff
1 CONC erent towns, during which not only the highereducational institutions but also the Danish "writing and arithmetic"schools were subjected to a searching examination. This might last forseveral days and consisted in the first place 
1 CONC in a check on thestandard of the knowledge of the pupils and the orthodoxy, knowledgeand teaching ability of the teachers. Where it was considerednecessary, instructions were given on curricula, text books andteaching methods.
1 CONT During his visits to the schools Winstrup also made himself acquaintedwith the moral qualifications of both the teachers and pupils and didnot hesitate to resort to severe punishment where more seriousshortcomings occurred. His cl
1 CONC ose solicitude for public education alsocarne to bear fruit. The situation in his diocese, which had left muchto be desired when he entered office, gradually underwent anappreciable improvement.
1 CONT As late as the 1650's there were, however, serious shortcomings whichin Winstrup's own opinion lay too deep to be dealt with by theintervention of individual persons, and which therefore demanded aschool reform. This also came to 
1 CONC pass in the 1650's, and the Bishop ofLund played an important role in it. The war with Sweden which brokeout a short time later, put an end for the time being to the practicalapplication of the measures decided upon.
1 CONT In the political sphere, Peder Winstrup always attached himself to Theroyal side and, in common with all his class, regarded the nobilitywith increasing disaffection. Of particular interest in thisconnection is the steady change w
1 CONC hich one can observe in his views onpolitical law.
1 CONT As a result of his views on society, based on the orthodox Lutheranthree-estate doctrine, he seems at first to have accepted theconditions of authority, such as they were, without much scruple.Dissatisfied with the small contribut
1 CONC ion of the nobility to the warsand their stronger and stronger grip on imperial government, andinfluenced by the growing mood in Europe in favor of a strongermonarchical power, his thoughts turned in an increasing degree towardsab
1 CONC solute monarchy.
1 CONT This change of attitude was not to appear in full until after thePeace of Roskilde. His anti-nobility attitude did not, however,prevent him from personal friendship with several of the leadingmembers of the Privy council and the n
1 CONC obility. As far as is known,when on a few occasions he played an important part in affairs, theparliamentary sessions also passed without disturbance.
1 CONT One last thing must be pointed out concerning Winstrup's behaviorafter the conclusion of the peace in 1658, a behavior which was muchcriticized especially from the Danish side; he looked on what tookplace first and foremost from a
1 CONC  ,religious and political law point ofview, and on these grounds he was completely convinced that he wasjustified in the attitude he adopted. No did he stand alone, since thewhole of the Scanian clergy gave him their full approval
1 CONC .
